1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,630
London. November 2005.

2
00:00:02,913 --> 00:00:06,813
The price of copper is at a record
high, and it keeps climbing.

3
00:00:07,113 --> 00:00:11,597
On the trading floors of the London
Metal Exchange, a rumour is spreading.

4
00:00:11,597 --> 00:00:13,112
Somewhere in this market,

5
00:00:13,112 --> 00:00:17,596
a trader working for the Chinese
government has bet that copper will fall.

6
00:00:17,596 --> 00:00:18,396
A huge bet.

7
00:00:18,580 --> 00:00:22,371
Well over a hundred thousand
tonnes of copper he does not own,

8
00:00:22,371 --> 00:00:24,755
promised for delivery before Christmas.

9
00:00:25,047 --> 00:00:27,147
And the trader has vanished.

10
00:00:27,447 --> 00:00:30,584
When reporters ask the
Chinese government about him,

11
00:00:30,584 --> 00:00:33,722
the first answer is that it
does not know who he is.

12
00:00:39,013 --> 00:00:44,363
His name was Liu Qibing. Inside China's
copper world, he was not unknown at all.

13
00:00:44,647 --> 00:00:47,709
He was the star trader of
the State Reserve Bureau,

14
00:00:47,709 --> 00:00:51,672
the agency that keeps the country's
emergency stockpiles of metal.

15
00:00:51,980 --> 00:00:55,283
Chinese newspapers would
later call him China's Leeson,

16
00:00:55,283 --> 00:00:58,105
after the trader who
brought down Barings Bank.

17
00:00:58,413 --> 00:01:02,863
His losses on paper would reach six
hundred and six million dollars.

18
00:01:03,180 --> 00:01:07,458
So how does a state employee end up
betting against the entire market?

19
00:01:07,458 --> 00:01:09,780
And when the bet goes wrong, who pays?

20
00:01:11,080 --> 00:01:13,823
Liu studied economics at Wuhan University,

21
00:01:13,823 --> 00:01:17,480
then joined the trading arm
of the State Reserve Bureau.

22
00:01:17,780 --> 00:01:19,596
The bureau's job was simple.

23
00:01:19,596 --> 00:01:24,005
Keep stockpiles of strategic metals
like copper, aluminium and zinc.

24
00:01:24,313 --> 00:01:29,763
Buy when prices are low. Release metal when
prices spike. Keep the country supplied.

25
00:01:30,080 --> 00:01:35,642
In 1995, the bureau sent Liu to London for
six months, to the London Metal Exchange.

26
00:01:35,642 --> 00:01:41,005
He helped build the computer link between
the exchange and his bureau in Beijing.

27
00:01:41,313 --> 00:01:46,413
He was, in a very literal sense, the
bureau's wire to the world's metal market.

28
00:01:46,713 --> 00:01:50,224
The LME is where the world
sets the price of copper.

29
00:01:50,224 --> 00:01:53,938
Miners, smelters, factories
and speculators meet there.

30
00:01:54,247 --> 00:01:58,935
And in 1998, Liu's centre began
trading on it with its own money.

31
00:01:58,935 --> 00:02:00,522
Liu placed the trades.

32
00:02:01,813 --> 00:02:06,538
Then China began to build. Roads,
towers, power lines, whole new cities.

33
00:02:06,538 --> 00:02:08,638
Every one of them needed copper.

34
00:02:08,947 --> 00:02:14,096
In 2003, copper was trading at around
sixteen hundred dollars a tonne.

35
00:02:14,096 --> 00:02:17,847
By the end of 2004, it was
close to three thousand.

36
00:02:18,147 --> 00:02:20,147
Liu had bet on the rise.

37
00:02:20,447 --> 00:02:22,621
According to a later academic study,

38
00:02:22,621 --> 00:02:26,548
his trades earned the state around
three hundred million dollars.

39
00:02:26,548 --> 00:02:28,722
He became the centre's chief trader.

40
00:02:29,013 --> 00:02:33,976
He also ran a quieter trade. Copper traded
in two places, Shanghai and London,

41
00:02:33,976 --> 00:02:36,521
and the two prices did not always agree.

42
00:02:36,521 --> 00:02:41,038
Foreign investors could not trade in
Shanghai. Liu could trade in both.

43
00:02:41,347 --> 00:02:44,197
So from around 2003, by
that academic account,

44
00:02:44,197 --> 00:02:48,782
he would typically buy in Shanghai and
sell in London, and pocket the gap.

45
00:02:48,782 --> 00:02:52,624
It meant that selling copper in
London was nothing new to him.

46
00:02:52,624 --> 00:02:54,297
It was part of his routine.

47
00:02:54,613 --> 00:02:56,925
And here is the detail that matters.

48
00:02:56,925 --> 00:03:01,421
By the middle of 2004, he was the
only authorised trader in his group.

49
00:03:01,421 --> 00:03:05,788
For more than a year, nobody else
in the room traded what he traded.

50
00:03:07,080 --> 00:03:11,262
As copper neared three thousand
dollars, Liu came to a conclusion.

51
00:03:11,262 --> 00:03:16,330
The boom had gone too far. Prices had run
ahead of reality, and they would fall.

52
00:03:16,647 --> 00:03:20,572
So he turned around. He started
betting that copper would drop.

53
00:03:20,880 --> 00:03:25,855
According to the Economic Observer,
people advised him to stop. He did not.

54
00:03:26,147 --> 00:03:29,883
In October 2004, copper fell
ten percent in a single day.

55
00:03:29,883 --> 00:03:33,881
To Liu, it looked like the turn
had arrived. He went further,

56
00:03:33,881 --> 00:03:38,797
selling large numbers of call options,
beyond what he was authorised to do.

57
00:03:39,113 --> 00:03:43,775
Here is what that means. Selling a call
option is collecting a small fee now,

58
00:03:43,775 --> 00:03:47,407
for promising to sell someone
copper later at a fixed price.

59
00:03:47,407 --> 00:03:49,648
If prices stay put, you keep the fee.

60
00:03:49,648 --> 00:03:53,038
If prices soar, you still have
to sell at the old price.

61
00:03:53,347 --> 00:03:56,197
There is no ceiling on
how much you can lose.

62
00:03:57,513 --> 00:04:01,073
On the LME, a bet that copper
will fall works like a promise.

63
00:04:01,073 --> 00:04:05,100
You agree today to deliver copper
on a future date, at today's price.

64
00:04:05,100 --> 00:04:09,652
If the price falls before then, you buy
cheap and deliver at the higher price.

65
00:04:09,652 --> 00:04:13,562
If it rises, you have to buy
expensive copper to keep your promise,

66
00:04:13,562 --> 00:04:14,613
or pay to get out.

67
00:04:14,913 --> 00:04:18,038
Through 2005, Liu's promise grew.

68
00:04:18,347 --> 00:04:22,924
By the summer, reports put it at around
a hundred and thirty thousand tonnes,

69
00:04:22,924 --> 00:04:25,718
most of it due on the
twenty-first of December.

70
00:04:25,718 --> 00:04:29,522
By some reports it later reached
more than two hundred thousand.

71
00:04:29,813 --> 00:04:31,376
To put that in perspective,

72
00:04:31,376 --> 00:04:36,182
all the copper sitting in the exchange's
warehouses at the time was about a hundred

73
00:04:36,182 --> 00:04:37,688
and forty thousand tonnes.

74
00:04:37,980 --> 00:04:42,205
He had promised to deliver more copper
than the whole market had in stock.

75
00:04:43,513 --> 00:04:47,404
In a market like the LME, a
position that big is hard to hide.

76
00:04:47,404 --> 00:04:52,363
Traders started to notice the selling.
And then they started asking a question.

77
00:04:52,680 --> 00:04:55,905
Does the bureau actually
have the copper to deliver?

78
00:04:56,213 --> 00:04:59,700
If it didn't, every trader who
bought against Liu would win.

79
00:04:59,700 --> 00:05:03,827
Because when December came, he would
have to buy copper back from them,

80
00:05:03,827 --> 00:05:05,513
at whatever price they asked.

81
00:05:05,813 --> 00:05:09,125
So, reportedly, they bought.
And copper kept climbing.

82
00:05:09,125 --> 00:05:14,155
Three thousand four hundred in January.
Three thousand eight hundred in September.

83
00:05:14,155 --> 00:05:15,688
Four thousand in October.

84
00:05:15,980 --> 00:05:19,177
Each step up cost Liu tens
of millions of dollars.

85
00:05:19,177 --> 00:05:24,357
Reports later said his supervisors pressed
him to double up rather than admit the

86
00:05:24,357 --> 00:05:25,157
losses.

87
00:05:25,113 --> 00:05:30,188
By October, his loss on paper had reached
six hundred and six million dollars.

88
00:05:30,480 --> 00:05:35,580
And in October 2005, Liu Qibing left
a farewell note, and disappeared.

89
00:05:36,880 --> 00:05:39,005
He did not stay gone for long.

90
00:05:39,313 --> 00:05:43,713
In October, he was detained in
Yunnan, in China's far south-west.

91
00:05:44,013 --> 00:05:49,223
But the market did not know that. For weeks,
the position just sat there. Unmanaged.

92
00:05:49,223 --> 00:05:52,138
With the delivery date
coming closer every day.

93
00:05:52,447 --> 00:05:56,674
In the middle of November, the story
broke in the international press.

94
00:05:56,674 --> 00:06:01,022
A Chinese state trader was missing,
and his short position was enormous.

95
00:06:02,313 --> 00:06:04,963
Then came the strangest part of the story.

96
00:06:05,247 --> 00:06:08,801
At first, the State Reserve
Bureau said it did not know him.

97
00:06:08,801 --> 00:06:10,518
Then it said he was on leave.

98
00:06:10,518 --> 00:06:15,197
Then it said he had acted alone, and that
any losses were his, not the state's.

99
00:06:15,513 --> 00:06:19,842
The bureau said it held one point three
million tonnes of copper in reserve.

100
00:06:19,842 --> 00:06:24,513
Market analysts thought the real figure
was nearer two hundred and fifty thousand.

101
00:06:24,813 --> 00:06:29,613
It began auctioning copper from the
stockpile, twenty thousand tonnes at a time.

102
00:06:29,913 --> 00:06:34,224
When one report said the bureau would
export two hundred thousand tonnes,

103
00:06:34,224 --> 00:06:36,763
copper fell ninety
dollars in five minutes.

104
00:06:37,047 --> 00:06:38,036
It did not hold.

105
00:06:38,036 --> 00:06:42,547
Copper hit a record of four thousand
two hundred and forty-three dollars.

106
00:06:42,847 --> 00:06:44,884
Some in London had a darker theory.

107
00:06:44,884 --> 00:06:49,599
An analyst at a French bank suggested the
whole thing might be the greatest bluff

108
00:06:49,599 --> 00:06:50,399
of all time.

109
00:06:50,613 --> 00:06:53,237
Perhaps the short position
was a smoke screen.

110
00:06:53,237 --> 00:06:57,856
Perhaps China wanted prices pushed up, so
it could sell its stockpile at the top.

111
00:06:57,856 --> 00:06:59,567
He admitted he might be wrong.

112
00:06:59,567 --> 00:07:03,788
But the theory showed how little anyone
trusted what they were being told.

113
00:07:04,080 --> 00:07:05,663
There was a bigger worry too.

114
00:07:05,663 --> 00:07:10,032
Britain's financial regulator warned the
exchange that if Liu had traded without

115
00:07:10,032 --> 00:07:13,636
permission, the bureau might not
have to honour his trades at all.

116
00:07:13,636 --> 00:07:15,820
The exchange answered that if it didn't,

117
00:07:15,820 --> 00:07:19,205
the brokers who had cleared those
trades would be on the hook.

118
00:07:19,513 --> 00:07:20,446
In other words,

119
00:07:20,446 --> 00:07:24,738
a missing man in China might leave
London's brokers holding the bill.

120
00:07:25,047 --> 00:07:27,928
As one London broker
told the New York Times,

121
00:07:27,928 --> 00:07:32,859
most of us don't know the full details,
or the size of any Chinese positions,

122
00:07:32,859 --> 00:07:34,972
or if the positions exist at all.

123
00:07:36,280 --> 00:07:40,593
In the end, the bureau did the only
thing it could. It found the metal.

124
00:07:40,593 --> 00:07:42,112
Around the delivery date,

125
00:07:42,112 --> 00:07:47,033
about fifty thousand tonnes of copper
turned up in LME warehouses in South Korea,

126
00:07:47,033 --> 00:07:48,855
most of it shipped from China.

127
00:07:49,147 --> 00:07:53,890
The rest of the position was
rolled forward into 2006 and 2007,

128
00:07:53,890 --> 00:07:55,847
and closed piece by piece.

129
00:07:56,147 --> 00:07:57,977
And copper kept going up.

130
00:07:57,977 --> 00:08:02,297
By May 2006, it was close to
nine thousand dollars a tonne.

131
00:08:02,613 --> 00:08:05,705
Even so, with physical
delivery, auctions and time,

132
00:08:05,705 --> 00:08:10,736
the bureau cut the loss from more than six
hundred million dollars to a hundred and

133
00:08:10,736 --> 00:08:11,888
forty-four million.

134
00:08:12,180 --> 00:08:14,381
Counting an earlier profit at home,

135
00:08:14,381 --> 00:08:18,155
the final bill came to nine
hundred and twenty million yuan.

136
00:08:19,447 --> 00:08:22,284
In 2008, Liu Qibing went
on trial in Beijing.

137
00:08:22,284 --> 00:08:27,327
The court found that he had used state
money for speculative trading abroad that

138
00:08:27,327 --> 00:08:31,172
was not hedging, against the
rules for state-owned companies.

139
00:08:31,480 --> 00:08:35,405
He was sentenced to seven years
in prison for abuse of power.

140
00:08:35,713 --> 00:08:40,048
His direct boss got six years. The
court found he had approved the trades,

141
00:08:40,048 --> 00:08:43,388
knowing the rules, and hidden
them when he left his post.

142
00:08:43,680 --> 00:08:45,645
There was a precedent. In 1997,

143
00:08:45,645 --> 00:08:50,905
staff at a state smelter in Zhuzhou had
also bet on falling metal prices in London,

144
00:08:50,905 --> 00:08:54,835
shorting zinc. They lost close
to one and a half billion yuan.

145
00:08:54,835 --> 00:08:58,638
The official responsible got
sixteen years. By that measure,

146
00:08:58,638 --> 00:09:03,455
an expert told the Economic Observer,
seven years was not especially severe.

147
00:09:03,747 --> 00:09:08,735
Some Chinese commentators said Liu was a
scapegoat for a system that had let one

148
00:09:08,735 --> 00:09:10,419
man trade alone for a year.

149
00:09:10,419 --> 00:09:14,347
Others said he was a rogue who
gambled with the nation's money.

150
00:09:14,647 --> 00:09:18,397
The bureau's trading arm was
barred from speculating again.

151
00:09:19,713 --> 00:09:24,025
And here is the final twist. Liu Qibing
believed copper was in a bubble.

152
00:09:24,025 --> 00:09:25,462
By one academic account,

153
00:09:25,462 --> 00:09:30,133
he had sold at an average of around three
and a half thousand dollars a tonne.

154
00:09:30,133 --> 00:09:34,026
In late 2008, as the global
financial crisis hit, copper crashed.

155
00:09:34,026 --> 00:09:39,057
By Christmas, it was trading at around two
thousand seven hundred and fifty dollars.

156
00:09:39,057 --> 00:09:40,913
Below the price he had sold at.

157
00:09:41,213 --> 00:09:44,763
He was right about copper. He
was just three years early.

158
00:09:45,047 --> 00:09:48,885
In markets, being early and being
wrong look exactly the same,

159
00:09:48,885 --> 00:09:51,547
right up until the day
you run out of time.

160
00:09:51,847 --> 00:09:55,772
Liu Qibing ran out of
time in October 2005.

